    "answer_text": "A fatty acid whose hydrocarbon chain has only single bonds between neighboring carbons is saturated, because the textbook defines a saturated fatty acid this way and says its hydrogen atoms are maximized [ev_008] [ev_001]. A fatty acid whose hydrocarbon chain has two carbon-carbon double bonds is unsaturated, because unsaturated fatty acids have one or more double bonds in the hydrocarbon chain [ev_002] [ev_009]. An unsaturated fatty acid does not have to contain exactly one double bond; it may have one or more double bonds, and if there is more than one it is called polyunsaturated [ev_002] [ev_008]. Fats are described as a stored form of energy [ev_002], and phospholipids comprise the membrane's matrix [ev_003].",
